x86_64-linux  There is nothing to compare here, by construction: a case that includes a C++ standard library header is compiled by clang against clang's own library and by cxx1 against this tree's `include/`, so the two objects hold the symbols of two implementations - `__ZNSt3__14swapB9nqe210106IiEE...` against `_ZSt4swapIiEvRT_S1_`. On the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. Behaviour is the oracle for these and run.sh asks it on all three machines; include-vector.nonames says the same about the same shape.
x86_64-windows  The same, and clang refuses the file outright for this target - it has no C++ headers to compile it against.
arm64-darwin  The same as x86_64-linux: clang's `<utility>` and this tree's are two implementations of one function.
